.TH GHOSTTY\-AGENT 1 "2026-09-22" "ghostty-agent" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ghostty\-agent \- terminal and desktop-window server for the Ghostty plugin for Final Fantasy XIV
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ghostty\-agent
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B ghostty\-agent
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR HOST:PORT ]
.RB [ \-\-token\-file
.IR PATH ]
.B \-\-open\-url
.I URL
.br
.B ghostty\-agent wg
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.B add
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-endpoint
.IR HOST [ :PORT ]]
.RB [ \-\-no\-qr ]
.br
.B ghostty\-agent wg
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.BR list " | " show " | " remove
.I NAME
.RB "| " forward " | " unforward
.RB [ tcp
.IR PORT " | "
.B udp
.IR PORT [ \-PORT "] | "
.BR sunshine ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ghostty\-agent
holds the terminal sessions that the Ghostty plugin shows inside Final Fantasy
XIV. The game connects to it over TCP and opens shells, ssh clients, tmux or
anything else; those sessions outlive the plugin reloading, the game closing,
and the machine the game runs on. Reattaching replays the recent output so the
terminal redraws exactly as it was.
.PP
It runs as you, not as a system service: every shell it opens is yours, it
writes only under your home directory, and it needs no privileges.
.PP
On Linux it can also be a headless Wayland compositor of its own. Programs
started into that compositor \(em a browser, an editor \(em become windows the
game can show as panels in the world, and X11 programs work there through
Xwayland. That backend is present only in builds made against wlroots 0.20;
.B ghostty\-agent
says which it is when it starts.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " HOST:PORT"
Address to listen on. Default
.BR 127.0.0.1:7777 .
The connection is authenticated by a token but is
.B not encrypted,
so only loopback is accepted, or a tailnet address while Tailscale runs on this
machine; anything else is refused (exit status 2). Reach the agent from
another machine through its own WireGuard
.RB ( "ghostty\-agent wg add" ),
through Tailscale, or inside an
.B ssh \-L
tunnel.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-insecure\-listen
Listen on the
.B \-\-listen
address even when it is on the network, in the clear. The agent says so when it
starts and logs every connection it accepts: anyone who can reach the port and
read the token has a shell as you.
.TP
.BI \-\-wireguard " auto|on|off|always"
The embedded WireGuard: a userspace WireGuard peer, no root or driver needed,
that the ordinary WireGuard apps connect to, and whose tunnel address reaches
this agent (and any ports
.B ghostty\-agent wg forward
allowed, on loopback only).
.B auto
(the default) starts it when
.I wireguard.conf
has a peer and Tailscale is not running here;
.B on
also when it has none, making the key if needed;
.B always
even with Tailscale;
.B off
never.
.TP
.BI \-\-wireguard\-config " PATH"
The WireGuard file, instead of
.I wireguard.conf
beside the token. Naming it turns WireGuard on.
.TP
.BI \-\-token\-file " PATH"
Where the shared token lives. Default
.IR ~/.config/ghostty\-agent/token ,
created with mode 0600 on first start if it does not exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-clipboard\-file " PATH"
A file used to exchange clipboard contents with the plugin.
.TP
.BI \-\-replay\-bytes " N"
How much recent output each session keeps for replay on reattach. Default 262144.
.TP
.BI \-\-term " NAME"
The
.B TERM
value given to shells. Default
.BR xterm\-256color .
.TP
.BI \-\-windows " NAME"
Which window-capture backend to use:
.BR auto ", " off ", " wayland ", " win32 ", " mac " or " test .
Default
.BR auto ,
which is
.B wayland
on Linux.
.TP
.BI \-\-wayland\-socket " NAME"
Name of the compositor's Wayland socket under
.BR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR .
Default
.BR ffxiv\-0 ,
or the next free
.BR ffxiv\- N.
.TP
.BI \-\-xkb\-layout " LAYOUT"
Keyboard layout for the compositor's seat.
.TP
.BI \-\-wayland\-render\-node " PATH|auto|gpu|none"
Which GPU the compositor draws with.
.RS
.TP
.B auto
The default. The card with the most free video memory among those driving
.I no
display and with at least 1 GiB free; software rendering when there is none.
This is the answer for the machine running the game: it never takes the card
the game is drawing on.
.TP
.B gpu
The best card there is \(em one driving a display included, and one whose free
video memory cannot be read included, which is every Intel GPU and any NVIDIA
card on a host without
.BR nvidia\-smi .
For a machine that is not running the game, where refusing the only card means
software rendering for nothing.
.TP
.BI /dev/dri/renderD N
That node, with no checks.
.TP
.BR none ", " pixman ", " off
Software rendering.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-wayland\-browser " CMD"
The browser command the compositor runs when the game opens a link.
.TP
.BI \-\-wayland\-browser\-profile " NAME"
A profile name passed to that browser.
.TP
.BI \-\-open\-url " URL"
Ask an already-running agent to open
.I URL
and exit, rather than starting a server.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "wg add " NAME
A new peer: prints the config for its WireGuard app (and a QR code for the
phone apps) and the plugin's
.B agent
line. The peer's private key is shown once and kept nowhere.
.B \-\-endpoint
names how the peer reaches this machine (UDP 51820 unless given; forward it on
your router);
.B \-\-agent\-port
the port the plugin line names.
.TP
.BR "wg list" ", " "wg show" ", " "wg remove " \fINAME\fR
The peers with their tunnel addresses and last handshakes; this end's public
key, port, addresses and forwards; dropping a peer, whose keys a running agent
wipes within seconds.
.TP
.BR "wg forward" ", " "wg unforward"
Let tunnel peers reach a port on this machine's loopback:
.BI "tcp " PORT\fR,
.BI "udp " PORT\fR[\fB\-\fIPORT\fR],
or
.B sunshine
for Sunshine's TCP and UDP ports. A running agent follows the file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ghostty\-agent/token
The shared token, mode 0600, written on first start. The plugin reads the same
file when the game and the agent are on one machine.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ghostty\-agent/wireguard.conf
The embedded WireGuard: this end's private key, its port, the tunnel
addresses, the forwards and the peers, in wg\-quick's format with
.B Name
and
.B Forward
added. Mode 0600. The running agent reads it again every two seconds.
.I wireguard.conf.status
beside it is what
.B wg list
shows.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/ghostty\-agent.service
A systemd
.B user
service. Start it with
.B systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now ghostty\-agent
and keep your shells alive between logins with
.BR "loginctl enable\-linger" .
It is deliberately not a system service: a system service has no desktop
session, and would lose the clipboard and every remote window.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GHOSTTY_WAYLAND_RENDER_NODE
Same values as
.BR \-\-wayland\-render\-node .
.TP
.B GHOSTTY_WAYLAND_SOCKET
Same as
.BR \-\-wayland\-socket .
.TP
.B GHOSTTY_XWAYLAND
.B off
leaves Xwayland out;
.B eager
starts it with the agent instead of with the first X11 client.
.TP
.B GHOSTTY_FFMPEG
The
.B ffmpeg
to use for short screen recordings; otherwise the first one on
.BR $PATH .
.SH EXAMPLES
Run it for a game on the same machine:
.PP
.RS
.B ghostty\-agent \-\-listen 127.0.0.1:7777
.RE
.PP
Run it on another machine, using whichever GPU it has, and let a gaming PC reach
it through the built-in WireGuard:
.PP
.RS
.B ghostty\-agent \-\-wayland\-render\-node gpu
.br
.B ghostty\-agent wg add gaming\-pc \-\-endpoint my\-home.example.org
.RE
.PP
Reach an agent on another machine without exposing it:
.PP
.RS
.B ssh \-L 7777:127.0.0.1:7777 that\-machine
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Asked to print usage, or stopped cleanly.
.TP
.B 1
Could not listen on the given address.
.TP
.B 2
A bad argument, a
.B \-\-listen
address on the network without
.BR \-\-allow\-insecure\-listen ,
or a WireGuard file that cannot be used.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR loginctl (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR wg (8)
.PP
The project, and the rest of the documentation:
.UR https://github.com/Spaceghost/ghostty\-dalamud
.UE
